Make the following changes to the log console. Do not change the existing overall architecture or unrelated functionality.

1. FIX HISTORICAL LOG PAGINATION / SCROLLING
- Initial historical load should fetch only a limited batch of log entries.
- When the user scrolls to the TOP, fetch the previous 50 entries from the backend.
- Prepend the new entries to the existing list.
- Preserve the user's current scroll position after prepending.
- Do NOT automatically scroll the viewport to the newly loaded entries.
- Do NOT trigger repeated pagination requests while a previous request is still running.
- The user must manually continue scrolling upward to request the next page.
- Verify this with a long dummy log file containing substantially more than 50 entries.
- Specifically validate that loading previous entries does not cause the viewport to jump to the top or continuously trigger pagination until the entire file is loaded.

2. FRONTEND MEMORY / LARGE HISTORY
- Do not load the entire today's log history into the browser.
- Implement bounded rendering/virtualization or an equivalent mechanism so the DOM does not grow indefinitely.
- Keep a reasonable client-side window (approximately 500–1000 rendered entries).
- Older entries may be removed from the frontend window when necessary, but must remain retrievable from the backend using pagination/cursor/offset information.
- Never modify, truncate, or delete the actual log files.
- Make sure the pagination mechanism can continue retrieving older entries when the user scrolls upward.

3. SEARCH
- Search must NOT operate only on currently loaded frontend entries.
- Search must be performed against today's available log data on the backend.
- Search across all applicable pods for today.
- Return only matching log entries to the frontend.
- Highlight the exact matching text inside each returned log entry.
- Preserve the existing Regex and Case Sensitive options.
- Do not display the entire log when a search is active.
- Clearing the search should return to the normal paginated/live log view.
- Make sure searching works even for a matching entry that has not yet been loaded into the browser through historical pagination.

4. TODAY-ONLY LOG SCOPE
- Remove the "Live / Today" selector.
- Remove the "All Pods" selector.
- The console should automatically operate on today's logs only.
- Automatically combine/stitch logs from multiple pods chronologically.
- Do not add archive or previous-day log functionality.
- Live streaming and historical pagination must remain separate behaviors.

5. POD COLOR / RESIZE HANDLE
- Remove the current full-height colored vertical bar.
- Pod names must use the same black text color as normal log text.
- Use the existing separator/resize handle between the pod/log metadata area and the actual log message as the pod color indicator.
- Change that indicator into a pill-shaped vertical handle.
- Normal state: thin pill.
- Hover/drag state: increase the thickness while retaining the same pod-specific color.
- It must remain a functional horizontal resize handle.
- Do not color the entire row or entire log message based on pod.
- Only the resize handle should carry the pod color.

6. CONSISTENT LOG COLLAPSING
- All multiline log entries must use the same collapsed preview behavior.
- Maximum collapsed preview = 4 lines.
- If a log entry exceeds 4 lines, collapse it.
- Keep expand/collapse behavior consistent across all log types.
- Do not use different thresholds such as 3, 5, or 7 lines depending on the log content.

7. VALIDATION
After implementation, test all of the following:
- Initial load does not load the entire dummy log.
- Scrolling to the top loads exactly the next historical page.
- The viewport does not jump while historical entries are prepended.
- Additional pages are loaded only when the user reaches the top again.
- Pagination does not automatically continue until EOF.
- Large historical logs do not cause uncontrolled DOM/memory growth.
- Search finds entries that are outside the currently loaded frontend page.
- Search displays only matching entries.
- Search terms are visibly highlighted.
- Multiple pods are included in today's search.
- Four-line collapse behavior is consistent.
- Pod color appears only on the pill-shaped resize handle.
- Resize functionality still works normally.
- No existing live-streaming functionality is broken.

Before modifying code, inspect the current pagination, scroll, search, log rendering, and resize-handle implementations and reuse the existing mechanisms where possible. Do not rewrite unrelated components.
